## 3. Production and Staging

I maintained separate application directories and database connection strings for Production and Staging.

| Item | Production | Staging |
|---|---|---|
| IIS site | `EmployeeWeb` | `EmployeeWebStaging` |
| Application pool | Existing Production pool | `EmployeeWebStagingPool` |
| Application directory | `C:\WebApp\Production` | `C:\WebApp\Staging` |
| Source directory | `C:\WebApp\EmployeeWeb` | `C:\WebApp\EmployeeWeb-Staging` |
| Database | `WebAppDB` | `WebAppDB_Staging` |

The Staging application was built and published using the .NET CLI.

Published output:
`C:\WebApp\Publish-StagingDB`

Before deployment, I created a backup of the existing Staging directory:

`C:\WebApp\Staging-backup-20261026-160641`

I stopped the Staging application pool, copied the published files into the Staging directory, and restarted the application pool.

I verified that the Staging website and its application pool returned to the `Started` state. I also accessed the application through `http://localhost:8081`.

Production was not intentionally modified during this deployment.

## 4. Active Directory Role-Based Access Control

I implemented and tested different permissions for existing Active Directory groups.

| AD group | Intended permissions | Test result |
|---|---|---|
| `DEV\WebAppReaders` | View the employee directory only | Passed |
| `DEV\WebAppEditors` | View employees and update department information | Passed |
| `DEV\WebAppAdmins` | View employees and execute database backups, but not edit departments | Passed |

The department update feature restricts available values to:

- `IT`
- `Sales`
- `Finance`

The Staging application validates submitted department values and uses a parameterized SQL update.

The department update endpoint also includes an authorization check and anti-forgery validation.

## 5. Database Integration and Testing

The Staging application uses the following connection string database setting:

`Database=WebAppDB_Staging;`

The Production application source uses:

`Database=WebAppDB;`

I tested the department update feature from `DEV01` using `testdev2`, a member of the Editor group.

I then verified that the updated department information was visible from a separate session using `testdev`.

This confirmed that the Staging application could update database records and that another session could observe the change.

I also inspected both source files to confirm that Production and Staging specify different databases.

Note: This verified the configured database separation. A direct comparison of Production and Staging records was not performed as part of the final check.

## 6. Database Backup

I implemented and tested a backup operation restricted to `DEV\WebAppAdmins`.

The Staging backup configuration uses:

- Database: `WebAppDB_Staging`
- Destination: `C:\Shares\LabShare\SQLBackups`
- File naming format: `WebAppDB_Staging_YYYYMMDD_HHMMSS.bak`

I signed in with an Admin account and successfully executed the backup through the Staging web application.

I then checked the destination directory in File Explorer and confirmed that a `.bak` file had been created.

The Admin account was also tested to ensure it could not update employee department information.

## 7. SMB File Sharing

The lab also included an SMB file share on SQL01.

Root path:

`C:\Shares\LabShare`

Subdirectories:

- `AppFiles`
- `SQLBackups`

The share and NTFS permissions were configured to support different access levels for the existing storage groups.

The storage access tests confirmed that the read-only test account could browse the share but could not create folders, while the modify test account could create, edit, and delete files.

The end-to-end AppFiles workflow through the Staging web application was not fully revalidated during the final deployment session.

## 8. Security and Deployment Practices

During this lab, I practiced the following operational procedures:

- Separating Production and Staging application directories.
- Using different database connection strings for each environment.
- Backing up the current Staging directory before deployment.
- Stopping only the Staging application pool during deployment.
- Preserving the existing Production application during Staging changes.
- Restricting application operations using Active Directory group membership.
- Validating input before updating database records.
- Using anti-forgery validation for state-changing HTTP requests.
- Verifying backup file creation after a successful backup operation.

